INT. OFFICE - BREAK ROOM - MORNING

Fluorescent hum. A vending machine, a motivational poster of a rowing team, a sad ficus in a fake-wood planter.

GERALDINE PRUITT-BELL (50s, regional director, pearls) sits at the head of the table, an agenda in front of her. She sips from a mug that reads WORLD'S OKAYEST BOSS. MILTON (30s, junior analyst, lanyard) enters with a pastry on a napkin and does not know where to look. HARPER (20s, intern, notebook) sits very still and writes down everything.

She taps her pen on the agenda. He sets the pastry on the table. She slides it back an inch. He slides it forward an inch. She sighs.

GERALDINE
Milton, we are here to discuss synergy.

MILTON
I thought we were here for the cake.

GERALDINE
There is no cake.

MILTON
There is a pastry.

GERALDINE
That is not cake.

Harper writes this down. Milton glances at her notebook. She tilts it away.

HARPER
Should I put "pastry" in the minutes?

GERALDINE
Put "synergy."

HARPER
Okay.

She writes. He eats the pastry. Geraldine closes her eyes and breathes in through her nose, counts to five, and opens them.

GERALDINE
We will now circle back.

MILTON
To what?

GERALDINE
To the thing we were circling.

He nods wisely. Harper nods too. Nobody has any idea what they are circling.
